00:34:23.850 --> 00:34:25.410
Yep.

00:34:25.410 --> 00:34:27.930
I had an interesting car situation.

00:34:27.930 --> 00:34:30.300
I think last time I told you,

00:34:30.300 --> 00:34:33.390
last time we went to visit Locke,

00:34:33.390 --> 00:34:36.720
there was a issue with
the rental car company.

00:34:36.720 --> 00:34:41.720
We had reserved a large SUV
in order to house everything

00:34:42.660 --> 00:34:44.490
that we were moving him in with.

00:34:44.490 --> 00:34:45.323
Yeah.

00:34:45.323 --> 00:34:46.590
Things that had been shipped down there

00:34:46.590 --> 00:34:48.360
and we're putting it all in.

00:34:48.360 --> 00:34:53.360
So we requested just a
mid-size SUV this time

00:34:53.370 --> 00:34:55.200
for me and Jessie and
Shepherd and then Locke

00:34:55.200 --> 00:34:56.307
to be able to take him around.

00:34:56.307 --> 00:34:57.867
And we show up at a rental car place,

00:34:57.867 --> 00:34:59.520
and just like last time,

00:34:59.520 --> 00:35:02.760
and I'm not gonna be one of those guys

00:35:02.760 --> 00:35:06.420
that complains about a
specific rental car company

00:35:06.420 --> 00:35:08.730
or an airline the way
that some people would,

00:35:08.730 --> 00:35:10.130
a Twitter following will do.

00:35:11.090 --> 00:35:14.897
I get it, but I'm not gonna
do that, I have lodged,

00:35:15.840 --> 00:35:18.540
I found out that it is lodge a complaint.

00:35:18.540 --> 00:35:21.270
You stick it in the crevice,
you cram it in there.

00:35:21.270 --> 00:35:23.730
I have lodged my complaint
with this company

00:35:23.730 --> 00:35:26.070
as I waited in line to find out that

00:35:26.070 --> 00:35:28.170
the reason that everyone
was waiting in line

00:35:28.170 --> 00:35:29.400
instead of just immediately going

00:35:29.400 --> 00:35:31.560
and getting into their car like usual,

00:35:31.560 --> 00:35:33.840
was that they didn't have enough cars.

00:35:33.840 --> 00:35:37.200
But what they did have was
a whole lot of minivans.

00:35:37.200 --> 00:35:38.033
Yes.

00:35:38.033 --> 00:35:40.440
I mean, minivans for days.

00:35:40.440 --> 00:35:41.820
Nobody wanted them.

00:35:41.820 --> 00:35:44.017
Well, I go to the lot, he's like,

00:35:44.017 --> 00:35:46.890
"Sir, you're in spot J23," or whatever,

00:35:46.890 --> 00:35:50.400
and I go over there and
that's when I realize that

00:35:50.400 --> 00:35:54.540
they've got no cars but 40 minivans.

00:35:54.540 --> 00:35:56.910
I don't know what they
think everybody in Miami,

00:35:56.910 --> 00:36:00.600
Miami, that's where we
were, wants minivans,

00:36:00.600 --> 00:36:03.273
'cause it doesn't feel
like a very minivan place.

00:36:04.380 --> 00:36:06.030
But, I gotta tell you-

00:36:06.030 --> 00:36:07.980
Can't be beat, cannot be beat.

00:36:07.980 --> 00:36:11.073
... I am just more and more
appreciating the minivan.

00:36:12.180 --> 00:36:14.220
It's funny because I'm moving
into this stage of my life

00:36:14.220 --> 00:36:17.170
where I don't really need one
because the kids are leaving.

00:36:18.090 --> 00:36:19.290
But I think I might be,

00:36:19.290 --> 00:36:22.230
our first empty nest purchase
might be just a minivan

00:36:22.230 --> 00:36:23.400
for me and Jessie.

00:36:23.400 --> 00:36:25.020
Dang, I know that ain't true.

00:36:25.020 --> 00:36:26.280
There's so many ports.

00:36:26.280 --> 00:36:27.330
That is not true.

00:36:27.330 --> 00:36:28.740
There's so many ports.

00:36:28.740 --> 00:36:29.573
Well, and you know what?

00:36:29.573 --> 00:36:32.850
They said, "Sir, we have either
a convertible or a minivan."

00:36:32.850 --> 00:36:34.050
A convertible minivan?

00:36:35.100 --> 00:36:37.723
The only choices they had,
convertible or minivan.

00:36:37.723 --> 00:36:39.790
Why didn't you get a convertible?

00:36:39.790 --> 00:36:44.220
Well, because there's a family of four

00:36:44.220 --> 00:36:45.450
that I'm gonna be taking
around in this thing.

00:36:45.450 --> 00:36:47.163
Yeah, okay, all right.

00:36:48.060 --> 00:36:51.123
Shepherd was not happy about this choice.

00:36:52.230 --> 00:36:53.190
He wanted the convertible.

00:36:53.190 --> 00:36:54.210
It was also,

00:36:54.210 --> 00:36:57.600
it was forecasting to be
raining most of the time,

00:36:57.600 --> 00:36:59.160
most of the days it was gonna be raining

00:36:59.160 --> 00:37:01.680
at least part of the time, so ...

00:37:01.680 --> 00:37:03.480
But when faced with a
minivan or a convertible,

00:37:03.480 --> 00:37:06.150
I mean, those are the
two ends of the spectrum

00:37:06.150 --> 00:37:07.800
when it comes to car choices.

00:37:07.800 --> 00:37:09.000
It should make it easy.

00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:09.833
I think you made the right choice.

00:37:09.833 --> 00:37:11.700
I felt like I was in a dream.

00:37:11.700 --> 00:37:12.600
This is not the kind of thing

00:37:12.600 --> 00:37:14.130
that you should have to
make a decision about

00:37:14.130 --> 00:37:14.963
at a rental car place.

00:37:14.963 --> 00:37:18.150
"Sir, we have the coolest vehicle

00:37:18.150 --> 00:37:19.770
and the most uncool vehicle.

00:37:19.770 --> 00:37:21.780
Which kind of person are you?"

00:37:21.780 --> 00:37:22.680
Form or function?

00:37:22.680 --> 00:37:24.810
That's not a dream, I'll tell you a dream.

00:37:24.810 --> 00:37:26.250
Oh, okay.

00:37:26.250 --> 00:37:28.830
Christy told me, we were on our trip,

00:37:28.830 --> 00:37:30.510
and I was like, "How'd
you sleep last night?"

00:37:30.510 --> 00:37:31.980
Because when you don't have kids around

00:37:31.980 --> 00:37:34.350
and you're just drinking your coffee

00:37:34.350 --> 00:37:35.250
and enjoying each other,

00:37:35.250 --> 00:37:38.010
you can ask things like,
"How'd you sleep last night?"

00:37:38.010 --> 00:37:40.260
And she's like, "Well
I had this weird dream

00:37:40.260 --> 00:37:43.230
that I had to pee.

00:37:43.230 --> 00:37:48.030
And actually, I was afraid,

00:37:48.030 --> 00:37:49.950
part of me was afraid I
was gonna pee in the bed.

00:37:49.950 --> 00:37:51.930
But I started peeing

00:37:51.930 --> 00:37:54.270
and then I realized I wasn't on a toilet,

00:37:54.270 --> 00:37:57.810
I was on a hamster cage."

00:37:57.810 --> 00:37:58.860
No problem there.

00:37:58.860 --> 00:38:00.150
Newspaper suck it right up.

00:38:00.150 --> 00:38:03.927
No, she said, "I was peeing
on a hamster, and it drowned."

00:38:04.980 --> 00:38:07.290
She killed a hamster with her piss.

00:38:07.290 --> 00:38:12.290
And she said, "It had a napkin over it."

00:38:13.560 --> 00:38:14.430
After it died?

00:38:14.430 --> 00:38:15.263
No.

00:38:15.263 --> 00:38:16.830
She slowly pulled a napkin over it?

00:38:16.830 --> 00:38:17.940
Don't want anybody to see that.

00:38:17.940 --> 00:38:18.925
She said, "I looked down

00:38:18.925 --> 00:38:22.707
and it had a sopping wet napkin over it."

00:38:24.330 --> 00:38:26.400
I guess it was snuggling in his bed.

00:38:26.400 --> 00:38:27.600
No, I don't believe in omens,

00:38:27.600 --> 00:38:29.650
but I do believe in dream interpretation.

00:38:32.370 --> 00:38:34.230
That's a dream, my friend,

00:38:34.230 --> 00:38:39.230
She killed hamster by
drowning it in her urine.

00:38:40.710 --> 00:38:43.050
That is some hardcore,

00:38:43.050 --> 00:38:45.090
I mean, I ain't yelling at her

00:38:45.090 --> 00:38:47.250
on the side of the road anymore,
you know what I'm saying?

00:38:47.250 --> 00:38:50.310
There's no joke yelling and you know.

00:38:50.310 --> 00:38:52.540
Are you the hamster?

00:38:52.540 --> 00:38:53.373
I could be the hamster.
I mean,

00:38:53.373 --> 00:38:54.206
are you on a hamster wheel?

00:38:54.206 --> 00:38:55.830
You're just endlessly running and working

00:38:55.830 --> 00:38:58.950
and grinding yourself down to nothing.

00:38:58.950 --> 00:38:59.783
And she's like,

00:38:59.783 --> 00:39:02.073
the only way to stop this
pattern is to pee on him.

00:39:04.200 --> 00:39:05.610
We had no interpretation.

00:39:05.610 --> 00:39:06.780
We were just too baffled by it.

00:39:06.780 --> 00:39:09.930
That's a dream, not
convertible minivan, but-

00:39:09.930 --> 00:39:10.763
All right.

00:39:10.763 --> 00:39:11.640
Well, here's the thing.

00:39:11.640 --> 00:39:13.890
When we picked up Locke,

00:39:13.890 --> 00:39:15.630
well, actually, Locke
came over to the hotel

00:39:15.630 --> 00:39:18.900
that we were staying in, which
was pretty close to campus.

00:39:18.900 --> 00:39:20.520
We got in pretty late

00:39:20.520 --> 00:39:22.500
and we were actually just expecting

00:39:22.500 --> 00:39:23.333
to see him in the morning

00:39:23.333 --> 00:39:24.300
'cause that's what we had talked about.

00:39:24.300 --> 00:39:27.570
But once we, cause it was
10 o'clock when we got in,

00:39:27.570 --> 00:39:28.740
he said, "I wanna see you guys."

00:39:28.740 --> 00:39:31.890
So he comes over and it's been what,

00:39:31.890 --> 00:39:35.310
a month or so since we dropped him off.

00:39:35.310 --> 00:39:37.410
Not long enough, in my opinion.

00:39:37.410 --> 00:39:38.820
If the fall break comes,

00:39:38.820 --> 00:39:39.653
the parents weekend comes real early.

00:39:39.653 --> 00:39:40.920
Too quick, too quick.

00:39:40.920 --> 00:39:45.030
But I think the whole idea of it is,

00:39:45.030 --> 00:39:47.550
I think this is multifaceted,

00:39:47.550 --> 00:39:48.750
but I think one thing is just like,

00:39:48.750 --> 00:39:50.490
we should get the parents
in there pretty early

00:39:50.490 --> 00:39:52.380
so they can check on these kids

00:39:52.380 --> 00:39:54.363
before they really derail themselves.

00:39:57.090 --> 00:39:58.890
They still got time to correct course

00:39:58.890 --> 00:40:00.750
before they've dug too big of a hole,

00:40:00.750 --> 00:40:03.450
which a freshman can tend to
do in that first semester.

00:40:03.450 --> 00:40:06.153
Well, yeah, It's like we moved,

00:40:08.070 --> 00:40:10.740
well, you know what,
I'll keep this anonymous.

00:40:10.740 --> 00:40:13.500
I might have moved one
of my kids into college

00:40:13.500 --> 00:40:14.940
for her second year,

00:40:14.940 --> 00:40:17.580
and then the next morning
we went back to see her

00:40:17.580 --> 00:40:18.911
and she was like-

00:40:18.911 --> 00:40:19.744
You talking about Lily?

00:40:19.744 --> 00:40:21.690
"Don't come in our apartment

00:40:21.690 --> 00:40:23.937
because there's vomit all over the rug."

00:40:26.280 --> 00:40:28.830
All right, she's living that life.

00:40:28.830 --> 00:40:30.000
It wasn't hers.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:30.833
Okay.

00:40:31.830 --> 00:40:33.060
Yeah, but somebody threw up.

00:40:33.060 --> 00:40:34.357
And Christy was like,

00:40:34.357 --> 00:40:35.997
"I brought that rug."

00:40:37.417 --> 00:40:38.907
"Just weeks ago."

00:40:39.877 --> 00:40:41.070
"Just last night."

00:40:41.070 --> 00:40:42.185
Oh, just last night.

00:40:42.185 --> 00:40:43.440
Yeah, 'cause we moved her in.

00:40:43.440 --> 00:40:44.973
Oh wow, it was a fresh rug.

00:40:46.170 --> 00:40:47.067
Just testing it out.

00:40:47.067 --> 00:40:48.030
Oh, my god.

00:40:48.030 --> 00:40:50.153
A little peeing on the
hamster, if you will.

00:40:51.990 --> 00:40:52.823
Oh, man.

00:40:52.823 --> 00:40:56.160
I'm throwing my whole family
under our bus in this episode.

00:40:56.160 --> 00:40:57.870
I'm gonna get strung out.

00:40:57.870 --> 00:41:02.870
Well, so the general report
is Locke is doing great.

00:41:03.030 --> 00:41:04.440
He is enjoying himself.

00:41:04.440 --> 00:41:06.210
He's doing well in school.

00:41:06.210 --> 00:41:07.740
But he's still very much Locke,

00:41:07.740 --> 00:41:12.740
which is a guy who is
filled with so many ideas

00:41:14.010 --> 00:41:16.290
that come at you in rapid succession.

00:41:16.290 --> 00:41:18.843
That's your fault, genetically.

00:41:22.290 --> 00:41:23.670
Here's the mistake that I made, right?

00:41:23.670 --> 00:41:25.440
I was kind of gearing myself up,

00:41:25.440 --> 00:41:27.990
and I'm gonna say this
by way of lesson learned

00:41:27.990 --> 00:41:31.200
and then maybe advice to
other parents out there.

00:41:31.200 --> 00:41:32.770
We talk often about

00:41:34.170 --> 00:41:36.810
the goal being to maintain
the relationship, right?

00:41:36.810 --> 00:41:40.050
Preserve the relationship,
that's paramount.

00:41:40.050 --> 00:41:42.690
It's more important that
you have the relationship

00:41:42.690 --> 00:41:46.080
than you have control, right?

00:41:46.080 --> 00:41:48.300
And there's an illusion of control.

00:41:48.300 --> 00:41:50.040
And even when your kid is at school,

00:41:50.040 --> 00:41:53.580
you still see them and even if they're 18,

00:41:53.580 --> 00:41:55.020
you still see them as your child

00:41:55.020 --> 00:41:57.930
that you have power and influence over

00:41:57.930 --> 00:42:00.600
and you care about them and
you want the best for them.

00:42:00.600 --> 00:42:03.400
And so when they come at you
with a bunch of ideas that,

00:42:04.387 --> 00:42:06.060
it was just way he was seeing things

00:42:06.060 --> 00:42:08.250
and things that he was gonna
thinking about doing and,

00:42:08.250 --> 00:42:11.550
there's a slow down, and I immediately,

00:42:11.550 --> 00:42:14.190
he triggered dad mode in a hard way.

00:42:14.190 --> 00:42:16.290
And I wasn't expecting it
'cause I wasn't even expecting

00:42:16.290 --> 00:42:20.040
to see him that night, I was
gonna see him the next day.

00:42:20.040 --> 00:42:24.180
And we very quickly return to a dynamic

00:42:24.180 --> 00:42:27.843
that often manifests itself
in our family, which is,

00:42:28.770 --> 00:42:30.660
Locke's talking at a
million miles a minute

00:42:30.660 --> 00:42:32.610
about all the things that he wants to do

00:42:32.610 --> 00:42:34.230
and all the things that he's thinking.

00:42:34.230 --> 00:42:37.260
And Jessie and I both
go into mom and dad mode

00:42:37.260 --> 00:42:40.740
where you're trying to give
advice and course correct.

00:42:40.740 --> 00:42:45.740
And it ended up being not
a great initial reunion.

00:42:45.930 --> 00:42:48.090
Now, it ended well that
night and we hugged,

00:42:48.090 --> 00:42:49.110
said I love you or whatever,

00:42:49.110 --> 00:42:52.083
we'll see you in the morning
and gonna go eat somewhere.

00:42:53.970 --> 00:42:55.640
But the first thing I did
when I sat down with him

00:42:55.640 --> 00:42:56.473
in the morning, I was like,

00:42:56.473 --> 00:43:01.473
"I wanna apologize for how I
was reactive to you last night

00:43:01.740 --> 00:43:03.240
when we talked,

00:43:03.240 --> 00:43:06.930
and I feel like we started
the trip off on bad foot

00:43:06.930 --> 00:43:11.400
and I don't want that to
be the dynamic between us.

00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:14.670
I trust you believe you,
love you," whatever.

00:43:14.670 --> 00:43:16.230
It actually was-
Pretty good.

00:43:16.230 --> 00:43:18.210
That was very helpful.

00:43:18.210 --> 00:43:20.550
Now, it's still the McLaughlins,

00:43:20.550 --> 00:43:22.290
and so everyone has an opinion

00:43:22.290 --> 00:43:24.330
and everyone is passionate
about their opinion

00:43:24.330 --> 00:43:26.763
and everybody thinks that
they're equally right.

00:43:27.990 --> 00:43:32.850
And Shepherd now, he's a
whole thing in and of himself

00:43:32.850 --> 00:43:34.143
as a 14 year old,

00:43:35.520 --> 00:43:39.930
which the best way I could
describe a 14 year old boy,

00:43:39.930 --> 00:43:42.090
and least the one that's in my house is,

00:43:42.090 --> 00:43:45.873
is as if someone suddenly got,

00:43:47.640 --> 00:43:50.943
they got turned on, slow
motion was turned on.

00:43:52.672 --> 00:43:55.740
It is like you're
watching a YouTube video,

00:43:55.740 --> 00:43:58.620
and then all of a sudden
you put it on half speed

00:43:58.620 --> 00:44:01.920
and everything slows down and gets deeper.

00:44:01.920 --> 00:44:03.960
That's what's happened to Shepherd.

00:44:03.960 --> 00:44:08.111
He's been stretched from a boy to a man.

00:44:08.111 --> 00:44:12.990
I was watching the video where
we took him out in the FJ

00:44:12.990 --> 00:44:15.330
during one of the vlogs and
kind of like let him drive.

00:44:15.330 --> 00:44:18.330
And his voice is so high,
he's sitting in my lap.

00:44:18.330 --> 00:44:20.763
At this point, Shepherd is six feet tall.

00:44:23.520 --> 00:44:24.600
His voice sounds like-

00:44:24.600 --> 00:44:26.190
He's my freaking height.

00:44:26.190 --> 00:44:27.630
His voice sounds exactly like me.

00:44:27.630 --> 00:44:28.650
So much so that,

00:44:28.650 --> 00:44:30.720
so Shepherd had his own room at the hotel

00:44:30.720 --> 00:44:33.240
and when we got there late,
restaurants were closed

00:44:33.240 --> 00:44:37.747
and so we just got some food
from the hotel and he was like,

00:44:37.747 --> 00:44:39.921
"Dad, can I call and get
some food from the hotel?"

00:44:39.921 --> 00:44:41.160
I was like, "Yeah."

00:44:41.160 --> 00:44:44.520
So he calls, and then I call from my room

00:44:44.520 --> 00:44:46.440
to get me something to eat.

00:44:46.440 --> 00:44:47.857
And then the first thing the guy said was,

00:44:47.857 --> 00:44:49.620
"Didn't you just call?"

00:44:49.620 --> 00:44:51.210
And I was like, "No, that was my son."

00:44:51.210 --> 00:44:53.010
So our voices sound the same now.

00:44:53.010 --> 00:44:54.870
So that little boy that was on my lap

00:44:54.870 --> 00:44:58.620
just a couple of years
ago is now this big man,

00:44:58.620 --> 00:45:00.660
but he's still a boy, right?

00:45:00.660 --> 00:45:01.493
He's a boy man.

00:45:02.670 --> 00:45:04.320
Right.
He's a moy.

00:45:04.320 --> 00:45:07.560
And he's moving in slow motion

00:45:07.560 --> 00:45:09.940
and he's entering into the
conversation like this.

00:45:09.940 --> 00:45:12.570
It's like he's becoming an ant.

00:45:12.570 --> 00:45:17.570
And boy, getting him up,
waking a 14 year old up.

00:45:21.487 --> 00:45:23.850
So we had to have a discussion,
a lengthy discussion

00:45:23.850 --> 00:45:27.240
about what sound he needed
to pick for his phone to make

00:45:27.240 --> 00:45:29.220
in order to wake him up,

00:45:29.220 --> 00:45:32.520
because he sleeps through multiple sounds.

00:45:32.520 --> 00:45:33.810
That there was an alarm.

00:45:33.810 --> 00:45:36.210
There was me calling his hotel room phone,

00:45:36.210 --> 00:45:37.500
There was me going down there and banging.

00:45:37.500 --> 00:45:39.727
And then finally, I was able
to get him up and be like,

00:45:39.727 --> 00:45:41.247
"Dude, we gotta go eat."

00:45:42.780 --> 00:45:45.300
But despite the fact
that he is become a moy

00:45:45.300 --> 00:45:48.600
and is moving in slow motion,
he is still a McLaughlin

00:45:48.600 --> 00:45:52.080
and that comes out when we
sit down and somebody just,

00:45:52.080 --> 00:45:57.080
it's so funny because this was
not the dynamic of my family.

00:45:58.170 --> 00:46:01.530
The dynamic of my family was

00:46:01.530 --> 00:46:03.300
my dad would kind of say something

00:46:03.300 --> 00:46:05.513
and everybody would just
kind of agree with it.

00:46:06.510 --> 00:46:08.880
That was sort of how my family worked.

00:46:08.880 --> 00:46:11.430
The dynamic of Jessie's family

00:46:11.430 --> 00:46:13.440
is that everybody has an opinion

00:46:13.440 --> 00:46:15.480
and everybody is very good at debating.

00:46:15.480 --> 00:46:17.010
And it's just like,

00:46:17.010 --> 00:46:18.480
hey guys, we're just talking

00:46:18.480 --> 00:46:22.650
about what we're eating right now.

00:46:22.650 --> 00:46:24.600
That doesn't have to become a debate,

00:46:24.600 --> 00:46:26.760
but the way they actually
show love to each other

00:46:26.760 --> 00:46:30.360
is by entering into debate.

00:46:30.360 --> 00:46:33.180
And so Jessie kind of has that in her.

00:46:33.180 --> 00:46:36.540
And then I can adapt to either situation.

00:46:36.540 --> 00:46:39.000
I can be like, I'm along for the ride,

00:46:39.000 --> 00:46:42.210
or I also have an opinion
and I can defend it.

00:46:42.210 --> 00:46:46.110
And so the dynamic that
has emerged in our family

00:46:46.110 --> 00:46:50.220
is that we go out to eat brunch,

00:46:50.220 --> 00:46:51.810
and we we're in this restaurant

00:46:51.810 --> 00:46:54.210
and we realize that
we're talking very loud

00:46:54.210 --> 00:46:55.560
and everyone's talking very passionately

00:46:55.560 --> 00:46:57.180
about whatever they're saying.

00:46:57.180 --> 00:47:00.840
And it's like, I feel like
people are now realizing

00:47:00.840 --> 00:47:03.210
how loud we're being and like-
Noticing.

00:47:03.210 --> 00:47:04.460
Definitely, definitely.

00:47:05.610 --> 00:47:06.443
But we actually,

00:47:06.443 --> 00:47:09.150
we had a chance to kind of
like have the meta conversation

00:47:09.150 --> 00:47:10.410
about the way that we,

00:47:10.410 --> 00:47:12.097
'cause the funny thing Locke said was,

00:47:12.097 --> 00:47:16.800
"It's just amazing how
quickly our dynamic returns."

00:47:16.800 --> 00:47:19.953
Like, this is the way
that we interact and-

00:47:21.540 --> 00:47:22.373
That's comforting.

00:47:22.373 --> 00:47:23.206
Well, he's like,

00:47:23.206 --> 00:47:24.930
"It's a little bit frustrating
but also comforting

00:47:24.930 --> 00:47:26.250
at the same time."

00:47:26.250 --> 00:47:28.950
But we talked about our
dynamic and then we were like,

00:47:30.510 --> 00:47:32.760
we need to believe the
best about each other.

00:47:33.630 --> 00:47:35.550
There's certain topics
that we might bring up

00:47:35.550 --> 00:47:36.750
that we disagree about,

00:47:36.750 --> 00:47:38.790
that, there's just no reason for us

00:47:38.790 --> 00:47:40.140
to talk about those things.

00:47:40.140 --> 00:47:41.550
It's like, this is about connecting,

00:47:41.550 --> 00:47:45.690
this is about figuring out
what you need at this juncture

00:47:45.690 --> 00:47:49.710
and us being there to be
supportive or whatever.

00:47:49.710 --> 00:47:53.760
And then we went to a football game.

00:47:53.760 --> 00:47:56.430
So I was excited about this

00:47:56.430 --> 00:48:00.180
because we just don't, now
that we're in California

00:48:00.180 --> 00:48:02.460
and we're so far away from NC State,

00:48:02.460 --> 00:48:03.570
if we still lived in the triangle,

00:48:03.570 --> 00:48:05.520
we'd probably be going
to NC State home games

00:48:05.520 --> 00:48:09.120
for not all of them maybe,
but I definitely would be-

00:48:09.120 --> 00:48:11.880
I would be going when you ask me to go.

00:48:11.880 --> 00:48:13.530
I'd have season tickets to the Wolf Pack.

00:48:13.530 --> 00:48:16.860
And I just love college athletics.

00:48:16.860 --> 00:48:20.070
I completely give myself
over to the mania,

00:48:20.070 --> 00:48:23.340
to the completely inexplicable mania.

00:48:23.340 --> 00:48:26.670
Especially that my only
connection to this school now

00:48:26.670 --> 00:48:29.273
is my son's going there, I am
giving them a lot of money.

00:48:33.217 --> 00:48:36.500
And they were also playing
Carolina in football.

00:48:37.950 --> 00:48:39.663
Gross.
And so I was like,

00:48:39.663 --> 00:48:41.940
Am I gonna have to go and watch Carolina

00:48:41.940 --> 00:48:45.810
beat another team that I've
now gotten dressed up for?

00:48:45.810 --> 00:48:48.630
We went to the student
store, we got the clothes.

00:48:48.630 --> 00:48:50.490
We were like, we're ready to go.

00:48:50.490 --> 00:48:52.590
I'm completely embracing this.

00:48:52.590 --> 00:48:54.183
And yeah, Carolina beat us.

00:48:55.470 --> 00:48:58.110
But I also was so into
it that I lost my voice.

00:48:58.110 --> 00:48:59.520
I'm still, that game was on-

00:48:59.520 --> 00:49:01.137
I noticed yesterday.

00:49:01.137 --> 00:49:02.550
... Saturday.

00:49:02.550 --> 00:49:03.420
You were real raspy.

00:49:03.420 --> 00:49:05.970
Well, what ended up happening
was there was this crazy,

00:49:05.970 --> 00:49:09.930
it was one of those things
where everybody started leaving

00:49:09.930 --> 00:49:11.760
because it looked like it was over.

00:49:11.760 --> 00:49:13.440
But then they started coming back

00:49:13.440 --> 00:49:16.350
and then there was this crazy
onsite kick that they got.

00:49:16.350 --> 00:49:17.850
But it turns out when
they watched the replay,

00:49:17.850 --> 00:49:19.110
the guy had gone out of bounds.

00:49:19.110 --> 00:49:22.290
But when they recovered the onsite kick

00:49:22.290 --> 00:49:25.650
before we realized that
there had been a violation,

00:49:25.650 --> 00:49:26.850
it was freaking nuts.

00:49:26.850 --> 00:49:29.130
And that was the point in
which a series of yells

00:49:29.130 --> 00:49:32.220
that I do not remember came out of me

00:49:32.220 --> 00:49:34.440
and caused me to like
lose my voice for several,

00:49:34.440 --> 00:49:36.210
not lose it, but be raspy.

00:49:36.210 --> 00:49:37.710
I'm still kind of there.

00:49:37.710 --> 00:49:38.543
Blew it out.

00:49:39.870 --> 00:49:42.360
So anyway, that was a really good moment.

00:49:42.360 --> 00:49:45.030
And Locke was like, "I had so much fun.

00:49:45.030 --> 00:49:48.270
I've never had that much
fun at a sporting event."

00:49:48.270 --> 00:49:53.270
We ended up having a very,
very sweet time as a family.

00:49:53.700 --> 00:49:57.217
And then when we were
leaving, Locke was like,

00:49:57.217 --> 00:49:59.610
"This is almost harder than
when you guys dropped me off."

00:49:59.610 --> 00:50:01.200
Ha.

00:50:01.200 --> 00:50:02.640
Got to meet his friends

00:50:02.640 --> 00:50:05.583
and the people he's
connecting with in his dorm.

00:50:06.690 --> 00:50:10.620
There was lots of hugging and
I love yous and it was good.

00:50:10.620 --> 00:50:14.970
So we did the thing, we did
the parents weekend thing,

00:50:14.970 --> 00:50:18.030
checked in, and I feel-

00:50:18.030 --> 00:50:18.863
Mission accomplishment.

00:50:18.863 --> 00:50:21.693
We were already in very close contact,

00:50:22.650 --> 00:50:24.480
in very frequent contact,

00:50:24.480 --> 00:50:26.640
much more than we were
even when he was here,

00:50:26.640 --> 00:50:27.990
when he was living with us.

00:50:28.860 --> 00:50:33.000
But that frequency has
even picked up since then

00:50:33.000 --> 00:50:34.470
with multiple texts a day.

00:50:34.470 --> 00:50:35.940
I'm sure it'll die back down,

00:50:35.940 --> 00:50:37.830
it kind of dies back down a little bit.

00:50:37.830 --> 00:50:40.380
But the relationship, which again,

00:50:40.380 --> 00:50:43.410
if my goal is preserving the relationship,

00:50:43.410 --> 00:50:46.413
maintaining that relationship,
then mission accomplished.

00:50:48.120 --> 00:50:51.690
You want to hear about the
person I met in the wilderness?

00:50:51.690 --> 00:50:52.523
Yeah.

00:50:52.523 --> 00:50:54.900
So we were staying,

00:50:54.900 --> 00:50:56.910
it's the same place we
stayed last year with,

00:50:56.910 --> 00:50:58.860
had the cabins, Glen Oaks.

00:50:58.860 --> 00:51:01.020
Shout out to Glen Oaks.

00:51:01.020 --> 00:51:05.670
It got cabins around the Big Sur River.

00:51:05.670 --> 00:51:10.230
So you're in the forest, you're
not on like the cliff's edge

00:51:10.230 --> 00:51:11.190
seeing the ocean

00:51:11.190 --> 00:51:14.550
from this particular point
in like the Big Sur Canyon.

00:51:14.550 --> 00:51:17.000
But it's absolutely amazing,
redwoods everywhere,

00:51:18.840 --> 00:51:20.700
these cute little cabins.

00:51:20.700 --> 00:51:21.533
Last time we went,

00:51:21.533 --> 00:51:25.050
they had shut down part of it
because there was a wedding.

00:51:25.050 --> 00:51:28.380
And so they do have weddings.

00:51:28.380 --> 00:51:32.850
We saw some people walking around in tuxes

00:51:32.850 --> 00:51:35.673
our first morning there where
we had a full day there.

00:51:36.870 --> 00:51:39.630
But we went down, we found
the Adirondack chairs

00:51:39.630 --> 00:51:43.470
and just hung out next to the river.

00:51:43.470 --> 00:51:45.630
And it's absolutely beautiful.

00:51:45.630 --> 00:51:50.630
I took my shirt off,
get a little vitamin D,

00:51:50.700 --> 00:51:51.900
very comfortable.

00:51:51.900 --> 00:51:53.010
How warm was it?

00:51:53.010 --> 00:51:57.330
And, it was probably 70 degrees.

00:51:57.330 --> 00:51:59.940
It couldn't have been more perfect.

00:51:59.940 --> 00:52:02.220
Which is chilly to take
your shirt off if you're me.

00:52:02.220 --> 00:52:04.650
But like the sun was just
giving you that nice,

00:52:04.650 --> 00:52:06.183
giving it the D man.

00:52:07.495 --> 00:52:11.817
And at certain points, I just have this,

00:52:13.890 --> 00:52:17.070
I like to be to myself, I
like to connect with Christy.

00:52:17.070 --> 00:52:19.050
And then at other points

00:52:19.050 --> 00:52:22.173
I feel this need to want to
connect with other people.

00:52:24.854 --> 00:52:26.340
It is so outta character for me,

00:52:26.340 --> 00:52:27.990
but sometimes I get to
a point where it's like,

00:52:27.990 --> 00:52:30.510
I like having conversations
with strangers,

00:52:30.510 --> 00:52:31.987
when we go to breakfast and I'm like,

00:52:31.987 --> 00:52:34.080
"Oh, where are you guys from?"

00:52:34.080 --> 00:52:34.913
I don't know.

00:52:34.913 --> 00:52:36.240
You think this is that
a character for you?

00:52:36.240 --> 00:52:38.010
Sometimes.

00:52:38.010 --> 00:52:40.200
Like when I go on a retreat, sometimes,

00:52:40.200 --> 00:52:42.360
oh, I don't wanna talk to anybody,

00:52:42.360 --> 00:52:46.170
but sometimes I'll get in that
mode, I'll get in that mood.

00:52:46.170 --> 00:52:49.440
I saw a couple come down
to the river below us

00:52:49.440 --> 00:52:53.280
and the guy was dressed up
like he was going to a wedding,

00:52:53.280 --> 00:52:55.830
I guess, this wedding that was happening.

00:52:55.830 --> 00:52:58.230
And then he was taking a
picture of his girlfriend

00:52:58.230 --> 00:52:59.550
and I was sitting there shirtless

00:52:59.550 --> 00:53:00.870
and I was just watching
them and I was like,

00:53:00.870 --> 00:53:02.100
you know what?

00:53:02.100 --> 00:53:03.570
I kind of want to go down there

00:53:03.570 --> 00:53:05.340
and be in the picture with her.

00:53:05.340 --> 00:53:08.100
I just think it would be funny.

00:53:08.100 --> 00:53:10.290
Well, yeah, funny is one word.

00:53:10.290 --> 00:53:11.313
Funny for me.

00:53:12.450 --> 00:53:13.410
Ding, ding, ding.

00:53:13.410 --> 00:53:15.120
Totally, you know how I am.

00:53:15.120 --> 00:53:18.543
I love the funny for me humor.

00:53:19.650 --> 00:53:20.677
And I'm telling Christy this, I'm like,

00:53:20.677 --> 00:53:22.500
"I'll also take a picture of them together

00:53:22.500 --> 00:53:23.730
'cause he's just taking pictures of her.

00:53:23.730 --> 00:53:25.380
He looks nice."

00:53:25.380 --> 00:53:27.540
And she's like, No, don't.

00:53:27.540 --> 00:53:29.400
You don't need to be social with them."

00:53:29.400 --> 00:53:30.540
Thank you, Christy.

00:53:30.540 --> 00:53:32.220
And they were a little far away,

00:53:32.220 --> 00:53:36.900
but he did turn and look
at me and I waved at him.

00:53:36.900 --> 00:53:38.850
Okay.
And he did not wave back.

00:53:38.850 --> 00:53:39.900
Yeah, 'cause it's a little strange.

00:53:39.900 --> 00:53:43.230
Which I took it to mean
that Christy was right.

00:53:43.230 --> 00:53:44.880
And then, she pointed the other direction

00:53:44.880 --> 00:53:47.370
and there's like a foot
bridge crossing the river,

00:53:47.370 --> 00:53:51.063
and this is 300 yards away.

00:53:51.960 --> 00:53:54.760
I know you're into football
so you know how far that is.

00:53:56.280 --> 00:53:59.040
You think it was three
football fields away?

00:53:59.040 --> 00:54:00.123
300 yards.

00:54:01.170 --> 00:54:02.003
Three football fields?

00:54:02.003 --> 00:54:03.000
Okay, fine.

00:54:03.000 --> 00:54:04.140
200 yards away.

00:54:04.140 --> 00:54:05.610
Because I mean, that's almost 1,000 feet.

00:54:05.610 --> 00:54:07.920
That's, I mean, some people
can't even see that far.

00:54:07.920 --> 00:54:09.570
Okay, how far is far enough

00:54:09.570 --> 00:54:11.738
that you can see someone
crossing a foot bridge?

00:54:11.738 --> 00:54:12.571
Well, imagine yourself out,

00:54:12.571 --> 00:54:14.220
the way I think about this-
This is a divergent,

00:54:14.220 --> 00:54:16.170
I imagine myself out-
100 yards.

00:54:16.170 --> 00:54:17.940
One football field.

00:54:17.940 --> 00:54:18.990
And I'm looking across,

00:54:18.990 --> 00:54:20.700
I mean, you played soccer, you know.

00:54:20.700 --> 00:54:21.640
They were one football field away.

00:54:21.640 --> 00:54:23.190
Yeah, that sounds a lot more reasonable.

00:54:23.190 --> 00:54:24.023
Okay.

00:54:25.200 --> 00:54:27.180
And it's people in tuxes,

00:54:27.180 --> 00:54:29.640
groomsman crossing this foot bridge.

00:54:29.640 --> 00:54:30.473
And then also-

00:54:30.473 --> 00:54:32.218
You found the wedding party.

00:54:32.218 --> 00:54:33.930
And I was like, oh, there's the bride.

00:54:33.930 --> 00:54:36.367
And I'm telling Christy, I'm like,

00:54:36.367 --> 00:54:38.698
"That's the one I want to talk to.

00:54:38.698 --> 00:54:40.048
I wanna talk to the bride."

00:54:41.580 --> 00:54:45.300
And I know that this is
putting Christy on edge

00:54:45.300 --> 00:54:47.463
and it's just funny to me.

00:54:48.600 --> 00:54:52.770
And she's rolling her eyes.

00:54:52.770 --> 00:54:54.900
And we are talking about the wedding

00:54:54.900 --> 00:54:57.600
and how it would be so
beautiful to get married here

00:54:57.600 --> 00:54:58.470
and stuff like that.

00:54:58.470 --> 00:55:00.660
We're almost talking ourselves
in renewing our vows,

00:55:00.660 --> 00:55:02.283
which I'm against.

00:55:03.420 --> 00:55:05.370
My vows are still very much intact.

00:55:05.370 --> 00:55:06.203
Okay.

00:55:06.203 --> 00:55:08.610
Even though they were very Jesus centric

00:55:08.610 --> 00:55:10.170
in one sense, you can make an argument

00:55:10.170 --> 00:55:11.460
that we need some new vows,

00:55:11.460 --> 00:55:14.610
but I also feel like we're doing great.

00:55:14.610 --> 00:55:15.660
There's nothing to renew.

00:55:15.660 --> 00:55:17.640
It's still going awesome.

00:55:17.640 --> 00:55:19.650
Isn't it just about the party?

00:55:19.650 --> 00:55:21.270
Well, we can have a party anyway.

00:55:21.270 --> 00:55:22.290
Okay.

00:55:22.290 --> 00:55:25.620
But then we're sitting there
in the chairs chilling out,

00:55:25.620 --> 00:55:28.080
and all of a sudden outta nowhere,

00:55:28.080 --> 00:55:33.080
pops this guy in a tux, the groom,

00:55:33.780 --> 00:55:36.540
with a photographer behind him.

00:55:36.540 --> 00:55:37.373
How close is he?

00:55:37.373 --> 00:55:38.400
How many football fields away is he?

00:55:38.400 --> 00:55:41.160
I would say he is five feet away from me.

00:55:41.160 --> 00:55:42.390
Okay, that's very close.

00:55:42.390 --> 00:55:44.250
I mean, popped out of nowhere.

00:55:44.250 --> 00:55:48.210
And then I saw behind the
photographer, the bride.

00:55:48.210 --> 00:55:51.210
And I'm like, oh, , it is on,

00:55:51.210 --> 00:55:53.310
it's time I get to meet this bride.

00:55:53.310 --> 00:55:55.470
And the guy's like-

00:55:55.470 --> 00:55:56.303
Why?

00:55:56.303 --> 00:55:58.710
He's like, "Would you
mind if we kick you out?"

00:55:58.710 --> 00:56:00.690
And he was like, "Okay, we're
gonna take wedding photos."

00:56:00.690 --> 00:56:02.040
And I was like, "Oh yes, yes, yes, yes."

00:56:02.040 --> 00:56:05.490
And I was shirtless, felt a little weird.

00:56:05.490 --> 00:56:07.860
I'm scrambling to put my shirt on,

00:56:07.860 --> 00:56:11.370
and he's looking at me strange.

00:56:11.370 --> 00:56:13.440
And then I was like,
well, I know I'm strange,

00:56:13.440 --> 00:56:17.160
but he's like, "Don't I
know you from the Internet?"

00:56:17.160 --> 00:56:18.742
And I was like, "Yeah."

00:56:18.742 --> 00:56:21.540
I was like, "I'm getting outta
here, you guys take over."

00:56:21.540 --> 00:56:22.620
He's like, "Let's get a picture."

00:56:22.620 --> 00:56:23.970
So I a picture with the guy.

00:56:23.970 --> 00:56:24.870
Shirt on, shirt off?

00:56:24.870 --> 00:56:27.203
Shirt is on at this point.
Okay.

00:56:28.530 --> 00:56:30.390
The bride's walking up, she's very quiet,

00:56:30.390 --> 00:56:32.250
she did not say anything.

00:56:32.250 --> 00:56:33.750
'Cause she's probably concerned.

00:56:33.750 --> 00:56:38.100
And I'm like, "You look
beautiful, congratulations."

00:56:38.100 --> 00:56:39.900
And then all of a sudden,

00:56:39.900 --> 00:56:42.540
there was a teenager there and a baby.

00:56:42.540 --> 00:56:45.030
And all of a sudden I'm confused.

00:56:45.030 --> 00:56:46.530
Is it Instant Family?

00:56:46.530 --> 00:56:50.010
And I started thinking
because it was on my brain

00:56:50.010 --> 00:56:51.783
that they were renewing their vows.

00:56:52.680 --> 00:56:55.717
And for some reason I came
this close to asking them,

00:56:55.717 --> 00:56:57.450
"Are you guys renewing your vows?"

00:56:57.450 --> 00:56:59.520
Wouldn't that be the
stupidest thing to ask someone

00:56:59.520 --> 00:57:00.700
who's getting married?

00:57:00.700 --> 00:57:01.653
Yeah, yeah.

00:57:02.850 --> 00:57:06.577
I didn't say it, because instead he said,

00:57:06.577 --> 00:57:09.240
"I'm from Naked and Afraid."

00:57:09.240 --> 00:57:10.290
And I'm like, "What?"

00:57:11.160 --> 00:57:12.690
Okay, all right.

00:57:12.690 --> 00:57:14.460
I was like, well we were already leaving.

00:57:14.460 --> 00:57:15.510
You're getting married.

00:57:15.510 --> 00:57:16.740
You don't have to drop the fact

00:57:16.740 --> 00:57:18.480
that you're in a reality competition show

00:57:18.480 --> 00:57:21.270
where you're naked and surviving.

00:57:21.270 --> 00:57:23.280
But it's like, okay.

00:57:23.280 --> 00:57:24.660
But he knew you from the Internet

00:57:24.660 --> 00:57:26.850
So he knew me, I guess
he was trying to get-

00:57:26.850 --> 00:57:27.767
What did he look like?

00:57:27.767 --> 00:57:32.767
He was a tall, strapping,
bearded man, middle-aged.

00:57:33.030 --> 00:57:34.170
Okay.

00:57:34.170 --> 00:57:35.430
Did he look anything like me?

